    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Cash-Out Refinance vs HELOC: How to Choose the Right Way to Access Your Home Equity</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've built equity in your home and want to access it, two products come up in almost every conversation: a cash-out refinance and a home equity line of credit. They're not interchangeable. They suit different financial situations, different timelines, and different uses of the funds. Choosing between them without understanding how each one works is how homeowners end up with the wrong product for their situation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Core Difference Between These Two Products</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A cash-out refinance replaces your existing mortgage with a new, larger loan. The difference between the old balance and the new one comes to you in cash at closing. From that point forward, you have one mortgage at the new rate and the new balance. A HELOC leaves your existing mortgage completely intact and adds a second lien on the property. The HELOC gives you a credit line you can draw from as needed, pay down, and draw from again during the draw period. Two products, two different structures, and two different cost implications depending on where rates are and what your existing mortgage looks like.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Cost Variable: Your Existing Rate</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's the calculation most homeowners skip when comparing these two products. A cash-out refinance replaces your entire mortgage at today's rate. If your existing mortgage carries a rate meaningfully below today's market, a cash-out refinance applies that higher rate to your entire remaining balance, not just to the equity you're accessing. The rate increase on the balance you already had adds a cost to the cash-out refinance that doesn't appear in the equity access comparison alone.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A HELOC leaves your existing mortgage untouched. You pay the HELOC rate only on what you draw, and only your existing mortgage rate on the original balance. In a higher rate environment, that separation is often the more cost-efficient structure for homeowners with low-rate first mortgages.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The calculation shifts in the other direction if your existing rate is at or above current market rates. In that case, a cash-out refinance that improves your first mortgage rate while also accessing equity can deliver better total economics than a HELOC.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">When Each Product Is the Stronger Choice</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A cash-out refinance is typically the better choice when your current mortgage rate is at or above today's market rate and a refinance improves that rate while also accessing equity, when you need a large lump sum at one time rather than flexible access to funds, or when you want the predictability of a fixed rate on the full combined balance.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A HELOC is typically the better choice when your current mortgage carries a rate well below today's market and you want to preserve it, when your funding needs are ongoing or uncertain in timing and amount rather than a single lump sum, or when you want access to equity as a financial safety net without paying interest until you draw.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Write down your current mortgage rate, remaining balance, and remaining term before you evaluate either product.</strong> Those three numbers determine how the cost comparison plays out.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate what a cash-out refinance would cost you on the full remaining balance at current rates.</strong> Compare that monthly payment to your current payment. The difference is the cost of the rate change on your existing balance, separate from anything to do with the equity you're accessing.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask your lender for a HELOC quote on the same equity amount.</strong> Compare the total monthly cost of your existing mortgage plus the HELOC payment against the cash-out refinance payment. The lower total is not always obvious without running both numbers.</li>
      <li><strong>Identify how you plan to use the funds.</strong> A defined one-time expense, a home renovation with a known budget, consolidating specific debt, these suit a cash-out refinance. Ongoing or uncertain needs, a business investment that will draw capital over time, an emergency fund, these suit a HELOC.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask your lender about draw period and repayment terms on the HELOC before you commit.</strong> HELOCs have a draw period during which you can borrow and repay, followed by a repayment period during which no new draws are permitted and the balance must be repaid. Understanding that structure before you open the line prevents surprises later.</li>
    </ol>

